# Commerce Insight — environment variables
# Copy to .env.local for development. NEVER commit .env or .env.local.
# Master Plan §19: no secret is ever prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_.

# ---- Site ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical origin. Used for metadataBase, sitemap.xml, robots.txt, OG URLs.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ---- Reviews (Phase 5) -----------------------------------------------------
# Published payload URL from tp-reviews-engine. Read SERVER-SIDE only.
# The visitor's browser must never contact Google. (Master Plan §13)
# REVIEWS_PAYLOAD_URL=""

# ---- YouTube ---------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTHING TO SET. Phase 16 Topic 9 built videos as a CURATED list: a teacher
# pastes a video link in the admin and the server keeps the eleven-character id.
# No API key, no channel id, and no server-side call to YouTube at all.
#
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY and YOUTUBE_CHANNEL_ID used to be listed here. They were
# removed because no code read them: an entry in this file is an instruction to
# an operator to set something, and setting those would have done nothing. That
# closes the second half of the Phase 15 F-1 drift (the first half was
# REVIEWS_PAYLOAD_URL, added to the deployment contract in Topic 7).
#
# If the channel feed is ever wanted, it is an additive change and the variables
# come back here AND in ENV_CONTRACT in src/lib/deployment-contract.ts.

# ---- Database (Phase 4) ----------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL connection string. Server-side only; never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# Local:  postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/commerce_insight?schema=public
# Neon:   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DB?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=""

# ---- Enquiry endpoint secret (Phase 4) — REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION ------------
# Keys the IP hash and signs the anti-spam form token. Must be >= 32 chars.
# Without it the IP hash is reversible and the form token is forgeable, so the
# app refuses to start in production rather than falling back to a default.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
ENQUIRY_SECRET=""

# ---- Admin panel (Phase 5) — REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION ------------------------
# Signs the admin session cookie. Must be >= 32 characters.
# Without it the session cookie is forgeable, so the app refuses to start in
# production rather than falling back to a default.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=""

# Create the admin account (never committed, never typed as an argument):
#   node scripts/create-admin.mjs "you@example.com" "Sir"

# ---- Enquiry notifications (Phase 5) ---------------------------------------
# Not wired yet: Commerce Insight has no professional email address and no
# sending domain, so there is nowhere authenticated to send to. The enquiry is
# persisted before notification is attempted, so this cannot lose a lead.
# RESEND_API_KEY=""
# ENQUIRY_NOTIFICATION_TO=""

# ---- Admin auth (Phase 5) --------------------------------------------------
# AUTH_SECRET=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MEDIA STORAGE (Phase 17)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where uploaded photographs live in production.
#
# ALL FOUR TOGETHER, OR NONE OF THEM.
#
# Unset on a developer machine: photographs go to .media-store/ on disk, which
# is correct there. Set on a deployment: they go to S3-compatible object
# storage. THREE of four is refused outright - a half-configured deployment that
# quietly fell back to local disk would lose every photograph on the next deploy,
# which is exactly the failure this design exists to prevent.
#
# Recommended: Cloudflare R2. 10 GB and free egress on its free tier, which at
# roughly 300 KB per stored photograph is about thirty thousand of them. The
# adapter speaks plain S3, so Backblaze B2, Wasabi, MinIO or AWS S3 all work
# without a code change.
#
# The bucket must be PRIVATE. Objects are served through /media/[key], never
# directly, so no public access is needed on the bucket at all.
#
# MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# MEDIA_S3_BUCKET=commerce-insight-media
# MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# MEDIA_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Optional. Defaults to "auto", which is what R2 wants. AWS S3 needs its region.
# MEDIA_S3_REGION=
